// A dashboard over a governed source.
//
// Nothing about the dashboard surface opts out of the model's governance. This
// file adds no scoping of its own beyond the given bindings: the row filtering
// lives on `sales` in orders.malloy, so every tile below inherits it, and the
// control row is generated from the givens the query references.
//
// The same holds for the hard boundary. Pointing this query at
// `orders_secured` instead (secured.malloy) would render the same grid for an
// admin or an allow-listed tenant, and return 403 for everyone else, because
// `#(authorize)` is evaluated when the query compiles and runs, not by the
// surface asking for it. A dashboard is not a way around an authorize rule.
##! experimental.givens
import { sales, region_suggest, status_suggest } from '../orders.malloy'
import { REGION, STATUS, MIN_AMOUNT } from '../orders.malloy'

// `sales` is imported to query, and the two suggest queries because a control's
// option list runs as a query against this dashboard's own model, so it has to
// resolve here. Importing the givens is what makes them bindable at all:
// Malloy's given namespace is per-file, so a control this file does not import
// cannot be sent to it.

#" Order volume and value, scoped by the controls above and by whatever the
#" model already allows this caller to see.
// `columns=12` and colspans that sum to 12 per row, the same grid the other
// example package uses, so the cards and the tiles end at the same place. The
// `# break` is what keeps the first tile from flowing in beside the cards.
# artifact { title="Governed Overview" } dashboard { columns=12 }
query: governed_overview is sales -> {
  // These three `where:` clauses are the control row. `sales` already applies
  // the same givens, so this adds no scoping; it is what tells the dashboard
  // which controls to render.
  where: region ~ $REGION and status ~ $STATUS and amount >= $MIN_AMOUNT

  // Three cards at 4 of 12. The labels come from the measures themselves
  // (orders.malloy), so they read the same here, in the notebook, and on any
  // axis they land on.
  aggregate:
    # colspan=4
    order_count
    # colspan=4
    total_amount
    # colspan=4
    avg_amount

  nest:
    # break
    # colspan=6
    # label="Order value by month"
    amount_by_month
    # colspan=6
    # label="Order value by region"
    by_region
  nest:
    # colspan=6
    # label="Orders by status"
    by_status
    # colspan=6
    # label="Order value by tenant"
    by_tenant
}

// This line is what this package needs and storefront does not. Curation is on
// here (`explores` plus `queryableSources: "declared"` in publisher.json), and
// under curation a model's queryable surface is what it exports: an import
// resolves at compile time but is not runnable. So the two option-list queries
// have to be re-exported, and once the list is explicit it replaces the default
// "everything top-level", which means the dashboard's own query belongs on it
// too. Leave `governed_overview` off and the grid stops loading; leave the
// suggests off and only the dropdowns come up empty.
export { governed_overview, region_suggest, status_suggest }
